# Chitinase III in pomegranate seeds (<em>Punica granatum</em> Linn.): a high-capacity calcium-binding protein in amyloplasts

## abstract
Chitinases are a class of ubiquitous proteins that are widely distributed in plants. Defense is the major natural role for chitinases, primarily against fungal pathogens. Little is known regarding their non-defensive roles in seeds. In this study, a new class III chitinase from pomegranate seeds (pomegranate seed chitinase, PSC) was isolated and purified to homogeneity. The native state of PSC is a monomer with a molecular weight of approximately 30 kDa. This chitinase naturally binds calcium ions with high capacity and low affinity, suggesting that PSC is a calcium storage protein. Consistent with this idea, its amino acid sequence (inferred from cDNA) is rich in acidic amino acid residues, especially Asp, similar to reported calcium storage proteins. The presence of calcium considerably improves the stability of the protein but has little effect on its enzymatic activity. Transmission electron microscopy analyses indicate that, similar to phytoferritin, this enzyme is widely distributed in the stroma of amyloplasts of the embryonic cells, suggesting that amyloplasts in seeds could serve as an alternative plastid for calcium storage. Indeed, the transmission electron microscopy results showed that, within the embryonic cells, calcium ions are mainly distributed in the stroma of the amyloplasts, consistent with a role for PSC in calcium storage. Thus, the plant appears to have evolved a new plastid for calcium storage in seeds. During seed germination, the content of this enzyme decreases with time, suggesting that it is involved in the germination process.

Chitinase III in pomegranate seeds (Punica granatum Linn.): a high-capacity calcium-binding protein in amyloplasts
## SUMMARY
Chitinases are a class of ubiquitous proteins that are widely distributed in plants. Defense is the major natural role for chitinases, primarily against fungal pathogens. Little is known regarding their non-defensive roles in seeds. In this study, a new class III chitinase from pomegranate seeds (pomegranate seed chitinase, PSC) was isolated and purified to homogeneity. The native state of PSC is a monomer with a molecular weight of approximately $30k\textcircled{>}\textcircled{=}$ . This chitinase naturally binds calcium ions with high capacity and low affinity, suggesting that PSC is a calcium storage protein. Consistent with this idea, its amino acid sequence (inferred from cDNA) is rich in acidic amino acid residues, especially Asp, similar to reported calcium storage proteins. The presence of calcium considerably improves the stability of the protein but has little effect on its enzymatic activity. Transmission electron microscopy analyses indicate that, similar to phytoferritin, this enzyme is widely distributed in the stroma of amyloplasts of the embryonic cells, suggesting that amyloplasts in seeds could serve as an alternative plastid for calcium storage. Indeed, the transmission electron microscopy results showed that, within the embryonic cells, calcium ions are mainly distributed in the stroma of the amyloplasts, consistent with a role for PSC in calcium storage. Thus, the plant appears to have evolved a new plastid for calcium storage in seeds. During seed germination, the content of this enzyme decreases with time, suggesting that it is involved in the germination process.
Keywords: chitinase, calcium storage, amino acid sequence, ultrastructure, seed germination, amyloplast.
## INTRODUCTION
In contrast to vertebrates, plants possess no immune system. Instead, they respond to pathogen attacks by inducing the expression of a large number of genes that encode diverse proteins, many of which have a function in defense (Collinge and Slusarenko, 1987; Bol and Linthorst, 1990; Bowles, 1990; Dixon and Harrison, 1990; Linthorst and Van Loon, 1991). Chitinases are unusual among these induced proteins because, until recently, no substrate for these enzymes had been identified in plants, although chitin performs a structural role in fungal cell walls and arthropod cuticles, including those of insects, nematodes and crustaceans.
Chitinases (EC 3.2.1.14) hydrolyze chitin, a linear homopolymer of $\upbeta$ -1,4-linked $N.$ -acetylglucosamine (GlcNAc) residues (Collinge et al., 1993). In plants, they play crucial roles in various biological processes, such as protection from bacterial diseases, and chitinous polysaccharide degradation (Fukamizo, 2000). Expression of chitinases is induced by pathogens or exogenous chemicals, and the chitinases release oligosaccharide elicitors that induce the production of phytoalexins (Fukamizo, 2000; Onaga and Taira, 2008). Many plant seeds accumulate chitinases in the absence of any such external stimuli, and these are thought to be components of an arsenal of defensive compounds utilized by plants (Huynh et al., 1992; Rao and Gowda, 2008). The importance of these functions is underlined by the ubiquitous distribution of chitinases in all organisms. Chitinase expression is also under developmental control in certain organs and tissues, and appears to have an important function in early embryonic development (De Jong et al.,
1993; Wu et al., 2001), indicating additional non-defensive roles.
Plant chitinases are divided into five classes according to sequence homology (Van Damme et al., 2007; Taira et al., 2009). Class I chitinases consist of a sequence with a highly conserved main structure and a Cys-rich N-terminal domain that is usually referred to as a hevein-like domain or chitinbinding domain (CBD) (Gomez et al., 2002). Class II chitinases are structurally homologous to the main structure of class I chitinases but lack the Cys-rich domain. Class IV chitinases share homology with class I chitinases but are smaller because of four deletions. Enzymes of classes I, II and IV are included in family 19 of glycosyl hydrolases, according to the classification by Henrissat and Bairoch (1993). Chitinases of classes III and V share no sequence homology with enzymes of classes I, II and IV, but have distant sequence similarity to bacterial and fungal chitinases and belong to family 18 (Fukamizo, 2000). Recently, multiple forms of chitinases from several plant species have been purified and characterized, especially from seeds, including maize (Zea mays) (Huynh et al., 1992), soybean (Glycine max) (Gijzen et al., 2001), rye (Secale cereale) (Stressmann et al., 2004), rice (Oryza sativa) (Mizuno et al., 2008) and barley (Hordeum vulgare) (Shin et al., 2008), contributing significantly to understanding of the relationship between structure and function of this important protein family. However, whether other types of chitinases that play different roles from the known analogs exist in seeds or plants remains to be determined.
In this study, a new class III chitinase from pomegranate seeds (Punica granatum: pomegranate seed chitinase, PSC) was isolated and purified to homogeneity. Interestingly, this chitinase naturally binds calcium ions with high capacity (approximately 10 ions per molecule), indicative of a possible calcium storage function. Immunohistochemical and Western blot analyses indicated that, unlike other chitinases occurring in the ER and vacuoles, this protein is located in the amyloplast stroma of pomegranate seed cells, in which calcium ions are abundant, in agreement with its proposed calcium storage function. Further studies showed that PSC plays a role in seed germination that has not been previously recognized.
## RESULTS
## Isolation and characterization of PSC
PSC was purified from pomegranate seeds by $(N H_{4})_{2}S O_{4}$ precipitation and ion exchange and gel filtration chromatography (data not shown). Proteins fractionated in the purification steps were resolved by a chitinase activity assay and SDS–PAGE. After treatment of the purified protein sample with 2-mercaptoethanol, the SDS–PAGE results show a single band (Figure 1a) with an apparent molecular weight of approximately 30 000, indicating that the protein in the peak is purified to homogeneity and contains only one kind of subunit. The same protein electrophoretic migration was observed on SDS–PAGE under non-reducing conditions (data not shown), indicating a single type of subunit in the purified PSC and a lack of inter-subunit disulfide bonds.
Figure 1(b) shows an overlay of a size-exclusion chromatogram of the purified protein with a size-exclusion chromatogram of a set of protein standards. A plot of the log molecular weight versus the size exclusion elution coefficient $K_{a v}$ is shown in the inset to Figure 1(b). A molecular weight of 31 $000\pm1500(n=3)$ was estimated for the native protein, consistent with the molecular weight derived from SDS–PAGE, indicating that the purified protein is a monomer in its native state. The molecular weight of the purified protein was also determined by MALDI-TOF MS in positiveion mode. The mass profile of the native protein shows a predominant peak at $m/z29\ 389.8$ Da (Figure 2a) that represents a singly charged protein monomer $[M+\mathsf{H}]^{+}$ . No further peaks were detected beyond $m/z~36~000~\mathsf{D a}$ in the spectral analysis. Thus, the accurate molecular weight of this protein is $29389.8\mathsf{D a},$ a value in good agreement with the molecular weights predicted by SDS–PAGE and size-exclusion chromatography. This value falls in the molecular weight range of $25{-}40~\mathsf{k D a}$ reported by several authors for chitinases from other plant sources (Leah et al., 1991; Gijzen et al., 2001; Ohnishi et al., 2005).
## N-terminal amino acid sequence, LC-MS/MS analysis, and isolation of cDNA of PSC
To characterize PSC further, its N-terminal amino acid sequence was analyzed by automated Edman degradation. The amino acid sequence obtained was GDIAIYWGQNGGEGT. This sequence was entered into the Swiss-Prot database (http://www.psc.edu/general/software/packages/ uniprot/) using the BLAST program (http://www.ncbi.nlm.
correspond to MS/MS spectra A, B, C and D in Figure S4.
nih.gov/BLAST/blast_program_shtml), and produced no protein matches containing the same sequence, indicating that PSC is a new protein. Figure 3(a) shows a comparison of the N-terminal sequences of PSC and some well-characterized chitinases from other plants in the NCBI protein database (http://www.usc.edu/hsc/nml/lib-services/bioinformatics/ncbi_ protein.html). Of 15 residues, 12 were conserved and revealed $80\%$ sequence identity with plant chitinases of class III, indicating that the purified protein belongs to a chitinase class III family.
To obtain more information on the molecular identity of PSC, four amino acid sequences were obtained by LC-MS/ MS (Figure 3b and S1). These four sequences were checked against the non-redundant protein sequence database using the BLAST algorithm, and best matched plant chitinases of class III, further supporting the above conclusion. However, no complete match was obtained, indicating that the protein has never been previously identified.
Subsequently, an eight amino acid region within the determined N-terminal sequence was used to derive an oligonucleotide primer (with codon degeneracy) for use in RT-PCR. A major PCR product of approximately 1000 bp was obtained and was used as a template for the subsequent nested PCR. The complete nucleotide sequence is shown in Figure 2. The inferred amino acid sequence of the cDNA clone exhibits up to $70\%$ identity with some reported class III chitinases (Figure 4). Furthermore, PSC contains two conserved consensus regions from family 18, highlighted in red. A previous study has shown that the glutamate residue in the consensus regions is essential for catalytic activity (Terwisscha et al., 1994). This glutamic acid corresponds to Glu127 in PSC, consistent with its position in hevamine (Terwisscha et al., 1994). These results further confirm that PSC is a new class III chitinase that belongs to family 18. A signal peptide of 26 amino acids characterized by a high proportion of hydrophobic residues is also present, in agreement with previous reports (Krishnan et al., 1994). The deduced amino acid sequence of PSC contains 21 aspartic acid residues, which is the highest amount among all known class III chitinases (Collinge et al., 1993; Fukamizo, 2000). Results of isoelectric focusing experiments show that PSC is acidic, with an isoelectric point $(p/)$ of 4.60, very close to the pI value (4.41) predicted by the algorithm from the ExPASy Compute pI program using the amino acid sequence of the mature protein.
## Calcium binding analyses
To elucidate whether the purified protein contains metal ions, the content of several metals (Mg, Ca, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu and Zn) in the protein was determined by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS). A very high ion intensity for calcium was observed in this purified protein. Based on three independent experiments, the calcium content in the protein sample $(3.48~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ was $1443.02\pm~3.87~\upmu9~\mathsf{l}^{-1}$ . The stoichiometry of bound calcium per protein was calculated as approximately 10:1 based on the molecular mass of the purified protein ( $29389.8\mathsf{D a}\mathsf{)}$ and the atomic weight of calcium (40.08). Thus one molecule of PSC possesses approximately ten atoms of calcium. To confirm this conclusion, the purified protein was treated with EDTA or EGTA followed by calcium analysis by ICP-MS. The results show that the calcium content in the treated protein sample $(3.28~\upmu\upmu)$ is below detection limit under the same experimental conditions, indicating that the calcium ions bind to the protein with a low affinity, as previously reported for calcium storage proteins in animals (MacLennan and Wong, 1971; Smith and Koch, 1989; Lucero et al., 1998; Michalak et al., 1999). In contrast, other metals were below their respective detection limit $\lvert10\mathrm{\ng\ml^{-1}}$ sample solution), suggesting that these metals do not occur naturally in the protein. Moreover, nearly identical analysis results were obtained by atomic absorption spectrophotometry (data not shown).
The mass profile of the purified protein acquired by MALDI-TOF/MS shows a major peak at $m/z\ 29\ 389.8\ \mathsf{D}\mathsf{\Omega}$ a (Figure 2a). This peak decreases to 29 008.7 Da upon treatment with EDTA or EGTA (Figure 2b), a value that is in perfect agreement with the theoretical value (29 008.19 Da) for the mature protein containing 273 amino acid residues (Figure 4). The difference in molecular weight between treated and untreated protein samples is 381.1 Da, approximately equal to the mass of ten molecules of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ minus that of 20 molecules of $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ . These results suggest that binding of ten calcium ions to one PSC molecule results in the release of 20 protons. This is consistent with a previous report indicating that one mole of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ is bound to the COOH group of two acidic residues in calsequestrin, causing release of two molecules of $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ (Wang et al., 1998). Moreover, this MALDI-TOF/MS result confirms the above-mentioned ICP-MS result showing that one PSC molecule binds to ten calcium ions.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis shows that EDTA- or EGTA-treated PSC is non-crystalline, as indicated by the ‘bun-shaped’ curves shown in Figure 5(c). In contrast, sharp peaks were observed for control samples (Ca–Asp2 complex) and native PSC (Figure 5a,b), suggesting that calcium exists in these two samples in crystalline form. The above complex exhibits nearly the same XRD pattern as the native PSC sample, suggesting that $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ binds with PSC through the COOH group of Asp residues, consistent with the observations using MALDI-TOF/MS (Figure 2).
## Effect of calcium on the structure and stability of PSC
PSC is a high-capacity low-affinity calcium-binding protein. This raises the question of whether calcium has an effect on the structure and function of PSC. To answer this question, changes in protein conformation were examined by UV/Vis spectrophotometry upon treatment with EDTA, followed by mixing with a series of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ solutions at various concentrations. As shown in Figure S3, the EDTA-treated PSC shows maximum absorbance at $278{\mathsf{n m}}$ , attributed to aromatic residue side chains. The spectral difference between samples containing $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ and those treated with EDTA indicates a significant decrease in maximal absorbance at $278{\mathsf{n m}}$ (Figure S3, inset), indicative of a subtle change in the environment of aromatic residues due to $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ -induced conformational change. Moreover, the degree of change is a function of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ concentration $(2-14~\mathsf{m m})$ , confirming the above conclusion.
To shed light on the mechanism by which protein conformation is altered by $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ , the change in the secondary structure of the protein was determined by circular dichroism (CD) in the absence and presence of calcium (Figure S4). The CD spectrum of native PSC is characteristic of $29\%$ $\mathfrak{a}$ -helical content, $18\%$ $\upbeta$ -sheet formation and $53\%$ random coil. Removal of calcium from the native protein or addition of calcium to this protein results in no change in the CD spectrum of the protein under these experimental conditions, demonstrating that the presence of calcium has no effect on the secondary structure of the protein.
The effect of calcium on the stability of PSC was investigated by thermal denaturation experiments performed using a UV/Vis spectrophotometer to monitor transitions in the thermally induced protein conformation at temperatures from $10-75^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure 6). The apparent transition temperature of protein thermal unfolding $({T_{\mathrm{m}}})$ calculated from the thermal denaturation curve was $67.3^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for native PSC, and decreases to $62.2^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ upon removal of calcium from the protein. The difference in melting point between the two proteins is ${\mathsf{5.1}}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}},$ indicating that the calcium ions improve the stability of the protein.
## Effect of calcium on the chitinolytic activity of PSC
The chitinolytic activity of PSC was assayed by a fluorogenic assay using 4-methylumbelliferyl (4-MU) chito-oligosaccharide, a commonly used substrate (Chou et al., 2006). The assay continuously monitored the fluorescence of the 4-MU generated from specific cleavage of the $\upbeta$ -glycosidic linkage between 4-MU and GlcNAc. To determine the optimum temperature, the chitinolytic activity of PSC was monitored by fluorogenic assay at temperatures between 5 and $65^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . As shown in Figure 7(a), the chitinase activity of PSC is a function of temperature. The enzymatic activity increases gradually with increasing temperature and reaches its peak at $45^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , followed by a decrease at higher temperatures. This result suggests that the optimal temperature for this enzyme is approximately $45^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ in agreement with those previously reported for other chitinases purified from seeds (Chang et al., 1992; Lin et al., 1992). Treatment of the enzyme with EDTA resulted in a significant decrease in the optimal temperature to approximately $35^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.$ This finding supports the thermal denaturation result.
Similarly, using 4-methylumbelliferyl- $\cdot\beta-\mathsf{D}\cdot N,N^{\prime},N^{\prime}$ -triacetylchitotrioside $[4-M\cup(\mathsf{G l c N A c})_{3}]$ as the substrate, the optimal pH for PSC was determined to be between 2.5 and 5.0 (Figure 7b), similar to those of chitinases from tomato seeds (Solanum lycopersicum) (Wu et al., 2001), tamarind seeds (Tamarindus indica) (Rao and Gowda, 2008), rye (Secale cereale) (Yamagami and Funatsu, 1993) and mung seeds (Phaseolus mungo) (Wang et al., 2005). In contrast, the EDTA-treated PSC is active in a relatively narrow pH range of 2.5–3.5; beyond this pH range, the activity dramatically decreases. Native PSC is therefore more resistant to pH than EDTA-treated PSC, again demonstrating that the presence of calcium in the protein improves its stability.
The catalytic kinetics of native PSC, EDTA-treated PSC and PSC with $10~\mathsf{m m}$ of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ were compared at optimal pH and temperature using $4-M\cup(G|c N A c)_{3}$ as the substrate to investigate the effect of calcium on chitinase activity. The protein and substrate concentrations were $5.25~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and $10~\mathsf{m m}$ , respectively. As shown in Figure S6, the three samples exhibit similar hydrolyzing activity against the substrate $(\upnu_{0}=0.487\pm0.17~\upmu\mathrm{m}~\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ for native PSC with $10\mathrm{\mm}\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ , $\upnu_{0}=0.374\pm0.15~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}\min^{-1}$ for native PSC, and $\upnu_{0}=0.289\pm0.11~\upmu\mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ for the EDTA-treated PSC), indicating that the presence of calcium has no significant effect on the chitinase activity of PSC. The overall activities, $k_{\mathrm{{cat}}}/K_{\mathrm{{m}}},$ of the three protein samples are similar $10.116\pm0.003~\mathsf{M}^{-1}~\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ for the EDTA-treated PSC, $0.119\pm$ $0.007~\mathsf{M}^{-1}~\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ for native PSC, and $0.130\pm0.012\mathrm{~m}^{-1}\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ for native PSC with $10\mathrm{\mw\}\mathsf{C a}^{2+})$ . The presence of calcium in PSC is therefore unnecessary for the catalytic activity of this enzyme but affects protein stability. This finding is inconsistent with the earlier report by Stressmann et al. (2004), showing that $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ enhances the activity of chitinase isolated from winter rye (Secale cereale) by three- to fivefold.
## Distribution of PSC in pomegranate fruit
Protein extract was isolated from various tissues of pomegranate fruit and probed using anti-PSC antibodies. PSC was abundant in the pomegranate seeds, and to a lesser extent in the skin (Figure 8); however, no PSC was detected in the leaves or flesh even after longer exposures.
## Localization of PSC in pomegranate seeds
To obtain insight into the distribution of PSC in seeds, the chitinase activities of extracts from the husk and embryo of pomegranate seeds were analyzed under the same experimental conditions as mentioned above. The results are shown in Figure S7. Compared with the control sample (bovine serum albumin), both husk and embryo extracts show significant chitinase activity. However, the chitinase activity of the embryo sample was approximately 7.7-fold greater than that of the husk sample, indicating that PSC is localized mostly in the embryo of pomegranate seeds. A high chitinase activity from the husk sample may be due to the incomplete separation between the husk and the embryo.
## Subcellular localization of PSC
Immunofluorescence localization of PSC using secondary antibodies conjugated to the fluorophore fluorescein isothiocyanate indicated the presence of abundant proteis in the pomegranate seed embryos (not shown). To confirm this, ultra-thin sections of pomegranate seed embryos were examined by transmission electron microscopy. Amyloplasts with small starch granules were clearly observed in the embryo cells of all samples (Figure 9). No immunogold particles were present in the negative controls (Figure 9a,c), but the stroma of the amyloplasts contains a high density of immunogold particles (Figure 9b,d). This result is inconsistent with previous findings showing that many chitinases, like other plant defense proteins, are secreted into vacuoles, apoplasts or the extracellular environment (Punja and Zhang, 1993; Sahai and Manocha, 1993).
## Localization of calcium in pomegranate seed cells and pH measurement of amyloplasts
To shed light on the possible physiological role of PSC, transmission electron microscopy was used to detect the distribution of calcium in ultra-thin sections of pomegranate seed embryos. No precipitate was present in negative controls that were not treated with potassium antimonite (Figure 10a,b). In contrast, upon treatment with potassium antimonite as previously described (Slocum and Roux, 1982), calcium-induced precipitates were mainly distributed in the stroma of the amyloplasts and apoplasts (Figure 10c,d), while a very small amount of precipitates occurred in the cytosol and cell walls. Moreover, immunofluorescence localization of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ using the fluorophore Fluo3-AM likewise indicated the presence of abundant calcium ions in the amyloplasts of pomegranate seed embryos (data not shown). Consistent with the present observation, calcium is also present in the amyloplasts of root-cap cells of corn (Zea mays var.rugosa Bomaf) (Chandra et al., 1982).
To determine the pH value of amyloplasts of the embryonic cells, amyloplasts were isolated from pomegranate seeds (Figure S8) and the pH was directly measured as described previously (Fu et al., 2010). The amyloplast pH was in the range 5.8–6.1 after various germination periods (12, 24 and 48 h). Thus, at a pH of approximately 6.0, PSC is stable with all calcium ions in a bound state (data not shown).
## PSC is involved in seed germination
To obtain further information on the possible physiological role of PSC, changes in the protein during germination of pomegranate seeds was studied. Seeds were collected after various periods of imbibition for protein extraction, followed by Western blot analysis using antibodies. As shown in Figure S9, PSC is present at all stages of seed germination in markedly decreased amounts from day 0 to day 15 after imbibition, indicative of the degradation of PSC in germinating seeds. Thus, PSC appears to play a role in seed germination.
## DISCUSSION
Chitinases are a class of ubiquitous proteins that are widely distributed in plants and that can be induced in response to microbial attack. Compared with their vegetative counterparts, seed chitinases have received relatively little attention. This may be because the former are associated with defensive responses against pathogen attack or other types of stress. Our research focuses on chitinases from a non-vegetative organ, i.e. seeds. We identified a new chitinase (PSC), that we purified to homogeneity from pomegranate seeds as indicated by SDS–PAGE, MS results and a single N-terminal amino acid sequence (Figures 1–3). The native state of PSC is a monomer with a molecular weight of 29 389.8 Da (Figure 2a) and a pI of 4.60, consistent with values for other chitinases (Leah et al., 1991; Lin et al., 1992; Gijzen et al., 2001; Ohnishi et al., 2005). This chitinase is a new class III chitinase based on a search using its N-terminal sequence (Figure 3a) and those of four other proteins obtained by LCMS/MS (Figure 3b and S1). This was further confirmed on the basis of the inferred complete amino acid sequence of the cDNA clone, which shares up to approximately $70\%$ identity with some known class III chitinases from other plant sources (Figure 4).
Interestingly, PSC is naturally rich in calcium, with a stoichiometry of approximately ten moles $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ per mole of protein, as indicated by ICP-MS analysis. This result was supported by MALDI-TOF/MS (Figure 2) and atomic absorption spectrophotometry. Thus, PSC is unique among all known chitinases because it naturally binds to $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ with high capacity. Compared with other $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ -binding proteins found in plants, such as calmodulin, calmodulin-like proteins, calcineurin B-like proteins and $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ -dependent protein kinases, which tightly bind up to six molecules of calcium ions per protein molecule using a helix-loop-helix structure (termed the ‘EF hand’) (Strynadka and James, 1989; White and Broadley, 2003), the calcium content of PSC is much higher, suggesting that PSC is a calcium storage protein. Upon treatment of native PSC with EDTA or EGTA, all calcium ions were removed (Figure 2b), indicating that they bind to this protein with low affinity. Given that this new protein is rich in Asp and Glu residues (Figure 4), it is reasonable to believe that these acidic amino residues are responsible for binding of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ to the protein in a mode similar to that found in calcium storage proteins such as calreticulin, calsequestrin and binding protein (BiP) (Crofts and Denecke, 1998; Furuyama and Dzelzkalns, 1999). Consistent with this idea, the complex $\mathsf{C a}\mathsf{-A s p}_{2}$ exhibited an almost identical XRD spectrum to that of PSC (Figure 5), suggesting that the Asp residues in PSC are involved in calcium binding.
Binding of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ to the protein had almost no effect on the secondary structure of the protein (Figure S4), but it induced a change in its tertiary structure (Figures S3 and S5) similar to that reported for a calsequestrin-like protein from sea urchin eggs (Echinoidea) (Lebeche and Kaminer, 1992). This a change markedly affected PSC stability. For example, $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ removal from PSC results in a $5.1^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ decrease in melting point $(\overline{{I_{\mathrm{m}}}})$ (Figure 6), demonstrating that the presence of calcium enhances the thermal stability of the protein. As expected, upon removal of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ from the enzyme, the optimal temperature of PSC activity decreased and the optimal pH became narrower (Figure 7a,b), further confirming the role of calcium ions in the protein. Consistent with the present observation, many divalent metal ions such as $Z n^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ play the same role in the stability of other proteins (Herzberg et al., 1986; Hayek et al., 1998; Niederberger et al., 1999) that $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ does in PSC. In contrast, the change in the overall activity of PSC is minimal upon removal of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ from the native PSC or addition of $10~\mathsf{m m}$ of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ to this enzyme (Figure S6), indicating that $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ is irrelevant to the enzymatic activity of PSC. This observation is inconsistent with a significant influence of calcium on the enzymatic activity of chitinase in rye (Stressmann et al., 2004).
The occurrence of both a large amount of calcium in PSC and the pronounced abundance of PSC in the amyloplast (Figure 9) raises the possibility that PSC is a calcium storage protein in pomegranate seeds. If this is the case, we would expect that most calcium ions occur in the amyloplasts of the embryonic cells of pomegranate seeds. As expected, within the embryonic cells, only the amyloplast is rich in calcium ions (Figure 10c,d), suggesting that the amyloplast is a main organelle for calcium storage in pomegranate seed cells, playing a similar role to the vacuole in the leaves or other tissues (Moreau, 1987; White and Broadley, 2003). It is reasonable to believe that a relatively high concentration of calcium in the amyloplasts facilitates binding of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ to PSC. Consistent with this idea, at approximately 6.0 (the pH value of the amyloplasts), all calcium ions are still combined with
PSC (data not shown). These results support the view that PSC is a calcium storage protein in seeds.
What is the physiological role of this enzyme? The answer lies in experimental results showing that its content decreases during seed germination (Figure S9), indicating that PSC possibly plays a role in this process. Such behavior is reminiscent of the iron storage protein ferritin. Phytoferritin from legume seeds is also located in the amyloplast (Lescure et al., 1991), and its concentration decreases progressively during the first week of germination (Yang et al., 2010). Based on these results, it is possible that chitinase plays a defensive role in seeds before germination. Another possibility is a role in providing $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ to cells to regulate various physiological processes at the seedling stage, because calcium is required for seed germination in higher plants (Moreau, 1987). More evidence is required to elucidate the role of this enzyme in the above physiological processes.
In conclusion, PSC is different from previously reported chitinases in that it binds $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ with high capacity and low affinity, and is abundant in amyloplasts. Moreover, within pomegranate seed cells, calcium ions mainly occur in the amyloplast stroma, in agreement with a role for PSC in calcium storage. The presence of calcium stabilizes the tertiary structure of PSC but has little effect on enzyme activity. This particular chitinase is involved in seed germination.
## EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURES
## Extraction and purification of pomegranate seed chitinase (PSC) and amyloplasts
Protein purification was performed at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ or less unless otherwise noted. Pomegranate seeds (Punica granatum L.) $(500\ {\mathfrak{g}})$ were soaked overnight in distilled water, ground and homogenized in 1.5 litre of $50~\mathsf{m m}$ Tris–HCl buffer, ${\mathsf{p H}}7.0$ , containing $1\%$ polyvinylpyrrolidone. The resultant mixture was filtered using 200 mesh gauze, and the filtrate was centrifuged at $ 12000g$ for 10 min. The resultant supernatant was treated with solid ammonium sulfate $(30\%$ saturation) with stirring, and left to stand overnight. The supernatant was collected by centrifugation at $ 12000\ g$ for $40\min$ followed by addition of solid ammonium sulfate with stirring until $50\%$ saturation. After the mixture was allowed to stand overnight, the precipitate was suspended in $100~\mathrm{{ml}}$ of 50 mM Tris–HCl buffer, ${\mathsf{p H}}7.0,$ and extensively dialyzed four times against the same buffer. The crude protein was filtered through a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ membrane (Millipore, http://www.millipore.com/), and used for anion exchange chromatography in a DEAE/Sepharose Fast Flow column (GE Healthcare, http://www.gehealthcare.com) equilibrated with $50~\mathsf{m m}$ Tris–HCl buffer, pH 7.0. Protein fractions were eluted from the column using a linear gradient of $0{-}1.0~\mathrm{{~m~}}$ sodium chloride dissolved in the above buffer. The fractions containing PSC were concentrated and further purified using a Sephacryl S-100 HR gel filtration column (GE Healthcare) equilibrated with $50~\mathsf{m m}$ Tris–HCl buffer, ${\mathsf{p H}}{\mathsf{7}}.0,$ containing 0.15 M sodium chloride. Fractions containing PSC were pooled, dialyzed against $50~\mathsf{m m}$ Tris–HCl, ${\mathsf{p H}}7.0,$ , and stored at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ until use. The protein concentration was determined according to the Lowry method using bovine serum albumin as the standard (Lowry et al., 1951). Amyloplasts were prepared according to a recently reported method (Fu et al., 2010), and their pH value was determined as described previously (Fu et al., 2010).
## N-terminal amino sequence analysis and LC-MS/MS
The amino acid sequence of the N-terminus was determined using a PROCISE 491 protein sequencer (Applied Biosystems, http:// www.appliedbiosystems.com/) by automated Edman degradation as previously described (Yang et al., 2010). LC-MS/MS was performed as described previously (Ottens et al., 2005).
## Molecular weight determination
The exact molecular weight was obtained by MALDI-TOF/MS on a Bruker Ultraflex III MALDI TOF/TOF (Bruker Daltonik GmbH, http:// www.bdal.de) in refractive positive-ion mode. The sample was prepared in water/trifluoroacetic acid $(100{:}0.1\ \mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ . Data were collected between 10 000 and 100 000 Da.
The protein was analyzed on a Sephacryl S-100 HR gel filtration column ( $30~\mathsf{c m}$ long, $1.6~\mathsf{c m}$ inner diameter) in $50~\mathsf{m m}$ Tris–HCl, pH 7.0, 0.15 M NaCl. Absorbance at $280~\mathsf{n m}$ was determined at a flow rate of $0.25~\mathrm{ml}~\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . All samples were subjected to centrifugation at $10\ 000\ g$ for 5 min in a microcentrifuge prior to injection. The size-exclusion column was calibrated using the following globular protein markers (molecular mass in parentheses): conalbumin $(75~\mathsf{k D a})$ , ovalbumin $(44~\mathsf{k D a})$ , carbonic anhydrase $(29\ k D a)$ , ribonuclease $(13.7~\mathsf{k D a})$ and aprotinin $(6.5~\mathsf{k D a})$ . The void volume was determined using blue dextran-2000.
## Removal of calcium from PSC and determination of calcium content
PSC (1 ml, $5~\upmu\upmu)$ was de-calcified by first dialyzing against $50~\mathsf{m m}$ Tris–HCl, pH 7.0 containing $5~\mathsf{m m}$ EDTA $(1.0\ L)$ for $24{-}36~\mathfrak{h}$ (changing the solution three or four times) at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The resulting solution was dialyzed against the same buffer without EDTA for $24-36~\mathsf{h}$ (changing the solution three or four times) at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to remove the EDTA. The resultant protein sample was stored at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ until use. Quantification of calcium was performed by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) using an Agilent 7500ce mass spectrometer (Agilent, http://www.agilent.com) and atomic absorption spectrophotometry using an AA-6300 spectrometer (Shimadzu, http://www.shimadzu.com/).
## cDNA cloning of PSC
Total RNA was prepared from mature pomegranate seeds using Sepasol RNA I (Nacalai Tesque, http://www.nacalai.com/), according to the manufacturer’s protocol. First-strand cDNA was synthesized using AMV reverse transcriptase (Takara, http:// www.takara-bio.com/) with $1.5~\upmu\up g$ total RNA and oligo(dT) adaptor primer containing the sequence of the M13M4 universal primer. The resulting first-strand cDNA was used as a template for PCR amplification of a partial sequence of PSC and for ${\sf3^{\prime}}$ -RACE PCR using the M13M4 primer and degenerate primer 1 (TAYTGGGGNCARAAYGG), which was designed based on part of the N-terminal sequence of PSC (YWGQNG). Nested PCR was performed using the M13M4 primer and degenerate primer 2 (AACCTHGCHGGCCAYTGYGACCC), which was designed based on the partial sequence of PSC determined by LC-MS/MS (NLAGHCDP) (Figure 3b). PCR was performed using Blend Taq (Toyobo, http:// www.toyobo-global.com/). A 1000 bp fragment of the nested PCR product was excised from the gel and cloned into the pCR2.1 vector of the TOPOTA cloning kit (Invitrogen, http://www.invitrogen.com/). The DNA sequence of the cloned DNA fragment was verified by automated DNA sequencing (ABI PRISM 3100, Applied Biosystems, http://www.appliedbiosystems.com/).
$5^{\prime}$ -RACE was performed using a ${\mathsf{J}}^{\prime}$ -full RACE core set (Takara) according to the manufacturer’s instructions. The primers were designed based on the partial sequence of PSC (Figure 3a). Reverse transcription was performed using the ${\mathsf{J}}^{\prime}$ -phosphorylated RT primer (GTTGTCGATGAAGTC) using Prime script RTase (Takara) and total RNA of pomegranate seeds (see above). The resulting first-strand cDNA was self-ligated using RNA ligase to make circular DNA, and was used as a template for the PCR reaction. PCR and nested PCR amplification were performed using primers Sense 1 (5¢-GGTTG TTCGACAACGTTTGG-3¢) and Antisense 1 $\cdot5^{\prime}$ -CTGCATCG AGAT GGCTATC-3¢), and primers Sense 2 $\cdot5^{\prime}$ -CAATACTCTTCGGGTAACACC-3¢) and Antisense 2 $5^{\prime}.$ -CGTGAGGTAAACCTTTGCTG-3¢), respectively. The nested PCR product was excised from the gel and cloned into the T-vector (Takara, http://www.takara-bio.com).
## Circular dichroism, UV and fluorescence spectroscopy
Circular dichroism (CD) spectra were recorded on a PiStar-180 spectrometer (Applied Photophysics, http://www.appliedbiosystems. com) at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under a constant flow of nitrogen gas. The spectra represent the average of 6–10 scans. The UV absorption spectra of the protein solutions $(0.58\ m\mathfrak{g}\ m|^{-1}$ in $50~\mathsf{m m}$ Tris–HCl, ${\mathsf{p H}}{\mathsf{\ 7}}.0,$ buffer) were obtained using a Cary 50 UV/Vis spectrophotometer (Varian, http://www.varianinc.com/cgi-bin/nav?/) at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The fluorescence emission spectra of the protein solutions $(0.29\ m\ g\ m\mathsf{I}^{-1}$ in $50~\mathsf{m m}$ Tris–HCl, pH 7.0, buffer) were scanned using a Cary Eclipse fluorescence spectrophotometer (Varian). All measurements were performed in triplicate.
## Thermal stability and chitinase activity assay
Thermal stability experiments were performed using a Cary 50 UV/ Vis spectrophotometer to record the change in absorbance at $280~\mathsf{n m}$ at temperatures ranging from 10 to $75^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Mergny and Lacroix, 2009). Protein samples $(0.15\mathrm{\mg\ml^{-1}})$ in $50~\mathsf{m m}$ Tris–HCl buffer, ${\mathsf{p H}}{\mathsf{7}}.0,$ were heated in a water bath to the indicated temperatures in thin-walled glass tubes before measurement. The chitinase activity assay was performed using commercial artificial fluorogenic substrates as described previously (You et al., 2003).
## X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis
Each protein sample was lyophilized for X-ray diffraction. The XRD patterns were recorded using a Bruker D8 Advance $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffractometer with $\mathsf{C u}K\boldsymbol{\alpha}$ radiation. The patterns were collected from $10^{\circ}$ to $70^{\circ}2\theta$ with a step scan of $0.0198^{\circ}2\theta$ .
## Effects of pH and temperature on chitinase activity
The effect of pH on enzyme activity was investigated at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ over a wide pH range $\left(\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}\ 2.0-8.0\right)$ , and the effect of temperature on enzyme activity was evaluated at $\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}7.0$ by incubating the substrate at temperatures ranging from 4 to $65^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The enzyme solutions (final concentration $0.2\ \mathsf{m g\ m l}^{-1})$ were incubated with 4-MU(Glc$N A c)_{3}$ (final concentration $5~\mathsf{m m}$ ) in a $10~\mathsf{m m}$ acetate buffer $(\mathsf{p H4.5})$ for $1\mathfrak{h}$ . Reactions were terminated by addition of $1\mathrm{\ml}200\mathrm{\mm}$ glycine/NaOH buffer $\mathsf{\Lambda}(\mathsf{p H}\ 10.6)$ . Experiments were performed in triplicate.
## Protein extractions and Western blot analysis
All extraction procedures were performed at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ as described previously (Hannoufa et al., 1991). All samples were stored at $\boldsymbol{-80^{\circ}C}$ until used. Each protein sample was prepared with three replications for each time point.
Antibodies against purified PSC were raised in a male albino rabbit (1 kg body weight) as described previously (Patel et al., 2007). The presence of antibodies was confirmed by immunodiffusion studies via double immunodiffusion as described by Ouchterlony and Nilsson (1986). Western blotting was performed by transferring proteins from SDS–PAGE to nitrocellulose membranes, as described by Towbin et al. (1979).
## Localization of PSC in the organs of pomegranate seeds
The husk and embryo of pomegranate seeds were separated and each was homogenized in three volumes of distilled water. The resultant mixtures were allowed to stand for $30\mathrm{\min}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . After centrifugation $(10000g,10\mathsf{m i n},4^{\circ}$ , the resulting supernatant was used for measurement of chitinase activity.
## Subcellular localization of PSC and calcium
Samples were prepared as described previously (Delecolle et al., 1992). Sections $(5~{\upmu\mathsf{m}}$ thick) were cut with a glass knife on a Leica UC6 microtome (http://www.leica.com/) and mounted on glass slides. The sections to be used for immunodetection were blocked using PBS containing $35~\mathsf{m m}$ glycine for $10\mathrm{\min}$ . Anti-PSC rabbit antibodies (1:15 000) in phosphate buffer containing $0.02\%$ Tween (PBT) were added to the samples and incubated overnight at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ Any unbound antibodies were removed by washing three times with PBS for ten minutes each time. The tissue sections were then incubated with $10\ \mathsf{n m}$ colloidal gold-conjugated goat anti-rabbit IgG diluted 1:20 in PBT for 1 h at room temperature. After washing twice with PBT for over $10\mathrm{\min}$ , the samples were stained with neutral uranyl acetate for 5 min and observed under a New Bio-TEM H-7500 transmission electron microscope (Hitachi Ltd, http://www.hitachi.com/). The localization of calcium in pomegranate seeds was determined using an antimonate precipitation technique (Slocum and Roux, 1982).
## Chitinase content during seed germination
Pomegranate seeds with no cracks or other visible deformation were surface-sterilized for $15\mathrm{\min}$ in $25~\mathrm{m}|\:0.5\%$ sodium hypochlorite, after which they were rinsed five times with sterile water for 5 min each. The surface-sterilized seeds were then placed on Whatman filter paper (http://www.whatman.com/) in Parafilmsealed $100~\mathrm{{mm}}$ diameter Petri dishes (Pechiney Plastic Packaging, http://stopwaste.org/docs/final_pechiney.pdf) with sufficient sterile double-distilled ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ to moisten the seeds, and maintained in a dark environment to germinate for 15 days. After the indicated germination time, the seeds were submitted to extraction for $1\mathfrak{h}$ (at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ with $50~\mathsf{m m}$ Tris–HCl buffer $\left(\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}\ 7.0\right)$ containing $1\%$ polyvinylpyrrolidone (w/v) plus 1 mM phenylmethylsulfonyl fluoride (w/v). The extracted material was centrifuged ( $10\ 000\ g,\ 10\ \mathrm{min},$ $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ , recovered by lyophilization, and utilized for Western blotting analysis of endogenous chitinase level using anti-PSC rabbit antibodies.
